WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.300 position:63%
The world is headed for stagflation next
year.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:07.080 position:63%
That's according to the latest Bloomberg
survey of professional and retail

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.540
investors.
What's stagflation, anyway?

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.990 position:59%
It's a scenario when inflation remains
above average.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:17.370 position:58%
Even as growth slows, that would be a
nightmare for the Federal Reserve and

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:21.840 position:63%
other central banks that have spent most
of this year deploying jumbo rate hikes

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:26.130 position:58%
to rein in inflation.
In a stagflation scenario, that would

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:31.200 position:63%
mean that those rate hikes will have
been ineffective in curbing prices, even

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.550 position:63%
as economic demand slows.
About a third of investors are even more

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:40.230
downbeat and expect a deflationary
recession, which means that both

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:45.020 position:59%
economic output and prices fall.
Meanwhile, they see plenty of risks in

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.450
housing markets.
Most investors expect a 20 percent

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.280 position:58%
plunge in house prices in a major
economy globally, with Canada and the

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.220 position:63%
U.K.
seen as the most vulnerable to this risk

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:01.020 position:61%
overall.
One thing's for sure 2023 is a year for

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:01.980 line:92%
the pessimists.

